|~ `::boolean` and what is declared under it: its constructor and its
   refusal, and the verbs that run on a boolean, the negation and the
   two that join booleans.
   The rules it holds: [QSDSP#residence]. ~|

:boolean
  |~~ A boolean is `true` or `false`, the answer to a question about a
      value. A condition is a boolean and nothing else is truthy:
      `filter` and `every` take code that answers one, `if` takes one
      computed at the call, and any other value is refused there.

      It is written `true` or `false`, as JSON writes it, and ranks
      after null and before every number. The verbs that ask answer one,
      `eq`, `gt`, `has`, `contains`, `is`; `not` turns it over, `and`
      and `or` join two, and `allOf` and `anyOf` join the conditions of
      one value, `5 | allOf (gt 0) (lt 10)` answering `true`. After
      steps, `ok !| false` answers whether they stood.
      ::snippet~(::boolean | manifest) lists its verbs and refusals.
      ::link~(::any/if | doc) ::link~(::qlang/values | doc)
  ~~|
  ::kind
    | wraps |~~ A boolean, read as itself. ~~|
    | throws :PayloadKindNotTakenError
        |~~ `::boolean` reads its payload as the boolean it is; a payload
            of another kind is refused.
        ~~|
    | proves :isABoolean
        |~~ True is a boolean. ~~|
        ~(true | type | eq ::boolean)
    | proves :buildsABoolean
        |~~ The constructor of a boolean answers it. ~~|
        ~(::boolean#false | eq false)
    | proves :PayloadKindNotTakenError/refusesANumber
        |~~ A number is no boolean and is refused. ~~|
        ~(::boolean#1 !| get [~(type) /actualType] | eq [::boolean/PayloadKindNotTakenError ::number])
    | impl ::builtin{}

:boolean/not
  |~~ Answers the other boolean, `true | not` answering `false`, and so
      turns the condition before it, `filter ~(gt 1 | not)`. The subject
      is a boolean, and `not` asks nothing of a value of another kind:
      null, zero and an empty vector are each refused, so a value is
      asked whether it is null by `eq null`, and a container whether it
      is empty by `empty`. A condition is asked first and turned after,
      `eq 5 | not`, or asked at once by `ne 5`; given to `not` as a
      value, `5 | not (eq 5)`, it leaves `not` a subject that is no
      boolean, and is refused.
      ::link~(::any/ne | doc) ::link~(::vec/empty | doc)
      ::link~(::boolean/and | doc)
  ~~|
  ::fn
    | returns
        |~~ The other boolean: `false` for `true`, `true` for `false`. ~~|
        ::boolean
    | throws :SubjectNotBooleanError
        |~~ `not` turns a boolean: a condition is asked first and turned
            after it, `eq 5 | not`, or asked at once by `ne 5`; a value is
            asked whether it is null by `eq null`, and a container whether
            it is empty by `empty`.
        ~~|
    | throws :ArgsBeyondInputsError
        |~~ `not` takes no value: it answers the opposite of the boolean
            in the pipe, and a condition goes before it as a step,
            `5 | eq 5 | not`.
        ~~|
    | proves :turnsTrueFalse
        |~~ Not true is false. ~~|
        ~(true | not | eq false)
    | proves :turnsFalseTrue |~~ Not false is true. ~~| ~(false | not)
    | proves :turnsTheConditionBeforeIt
        |~~ A condition is turned after it is asked. ~~|
        ~([1 2] | filter ~(gt 1 | not) | eq [1])
    | proves :SubjectNotBooleanError/refusesNull
        |~~ Null is no boolean; it is asked for by `eq null`. ~~|
        ~(null | not !| get [~(type) /actualType] | eq [::boolean/not/SubjectNotBooleanError ::null])
    | proves :SubjectNotBooleanError/refusesANumber
        |~~ A number is no boolean, and `not` refuses it. ~~|
        ~(0 | not !| get [~(type) /actualType] | eq [::boolean/not/SubjectNotBooleanError ::number])
    | proves :SubjectNotBooleanError/refusesAConditionGivenAsAValue
        |~~ A condition given to `not` leaves it a subject that is no
            boolean.
        ~~|
        ~(5 | not (eq 5) !| type | eq ::boolean/not/SubjectNotBooleanError)
    | proves :SubjectNotBooleanError/refusesAnEmptyVector
        |~~ An empty vector is no boolean. ~~|
        ~([] | not !| type | eq ::boolean/not/SubjectNotBooleanError)
    | proves :ArgsBeyondInputsError/refusesAConditionGiven
        |~~ A condition given to the call is refused under the verb. ~~|
        ~(true | not (eq 5) !| type | eq ::boolean/not/ArgsBeyondInputsError)
    | impl ::builtin{}

:boolean/and
  |~~ Answers whether the subject and the condition the call gives both
      hold, `true | and false` answering `false`. Both are booleans: a
      subject of another kind is refused under the verb, and a condition
      of another kind under the input. The condition is computed against
      the subject, the boolean the step before answered, so it reaches
      neither the element nor the record that step was asked of: in
      `filter ~(gt 1 | and (lt 3))` the `lt` is asked of a boolean and is
      refused. The conditions of one value hold together by `allOf`,
      `filter ~(allOf (gt 1) (lt 3))`, and of one record the same way,
      `allOf /active (get /age | gte 18)`, three conditions or more among
      them. `or` answers whether one of the two holds.
      ::link~(::any/allOf | doc) ::link~(::boolean/or | doc)
  ~~|
  ::fn
    | takes :condition
        |~~ The boolean joined with the subject, computed against the
            subject.
        ~~|
        /0 ::boolean
    | throws :condition/KindNotTakenError
        |~~ The condition is a boolean, `true | and (5 | gt 3)`; the
            conditions of one value hold together by `allOf`.
        ~~|
    | returns
        |~~ `true` when the subject and the condition both hold, `false`
            otherwise.
        ~~|
        ::boolean
    | throws :SubjectNotBooleanError
        |~~ `and` joins two booleans, the subject and the condition: the
            conditions of one value hold together by
            `allOf (gt 1) (lt 3)`, and of one record by
            `allOf (get /active) (get /age | gte 18)`.
        ~~|
    | throws :ArgsBeyondInputsError
        |~~ `and` joins the subject with one boolean: three conditions or
            more hold together by `allOf`, `allOf (gt 1) (lt 9) (ne 5)`.
        ~~|
    | proves :holdsWhenBothHold
        |~~ True and true is true. ~~|
        ~(true | and true)
    | proves :isFalseWhenOneIsFalse
        |~~ True and false is false. ~~|
        ~(true | and false | not)
    | proves :computesTheConditionAgainstTheSubject
        |~~ The condition is computed against the subject, a boolean. ~~|
        ~(true | and (eq true))
    | proves :condition/KindNotTakenError/refusesANumber
        |~~ A condition that is no boolean is refused under the input. ~~|
        ~(true | and 1 !| get [~(type) /actualType] | eq [::boolean/and/condition/KindNotTakenError ::number])
    | proves :SubjectNotBooleanError/refusesANumber
        |~~ A subject that is no boolean is refused under the verb. ~~|
        ~(1 | and true !| get [~(type) /actualType] | eq [::boolean/and/SubjectNotBooleanError ::number])
    | proves :ArgsBeyondInputsError/refusesTwoBooleans
        |~~ A third condition is refused under the verb. ~~|
        ~(true | and true true !| type | eq ::boolean/and/ArgsBeyondInputsError)
    | proves :asksTheConditionOfTheBoolean
        |~~ The condition is asked of the boolean the step before answered,
            so a comparison of the element there is refused.
        ~~|
        ~([1 2 3] | filter ~(gt 1 | and (lt 3)) !| get [~(type) /leftType] | eq [::call/KindsMismatchedError ::boolean])
    | impl ::builtin{}

:boolean/or
  |~~ Answers whether the subject or the condition the call gives holds,
      `false | or true` answering `true`, and `false` when neither does.
      Both are booleans: a subject of another kind is refused under the
      verb, and a condition of another kind under the input. The
      condition is computed against the subject, the boolean the step
      before answered, as for `and`, so the conditions of one value hold
      by one of them through `anyOf`, `5 | anyOf (eq 5) (eq 10)`, and of
      one record the same way,
      `anyOf (get /role | eq "admin") (get /role | eq "owner")`, three
      conditions or more among them.
      ::link~(::any/anyOf | doc) ::link~(::boolean/and | doc)
  ~~|
  ::fn
    | takes :condition
        |~~ The boolean joined with the subject, computed against the
            subject.
        ~~|
        /0 ::boolean
    | throws :condition/KindNotTakenError
        |~~ The condition is a boolean, `false | or (5 | gt 3)`; the
            conditions of one value hold together by `anyOf`.
        ~~|
    | returns
        |~~ `true` when the subject or the condition holds, `false`
            otherwise.
        ~~|
        ::boolean
    | throws :SubjectNotBooleanError
        |~~ `or` joins two booleans, the subject and the condition: the
            conditions of one value hold by one of them through
            `anyOf (eq 5) (eq 10)`, and of one record by
            `anyOf (get /role | eq "admin") (get /role | eq "owner")`.
        ~~|
    | throws :ArgsBeyondInputsError
        |~~ `or` joins the subject with one boolean: three conditions or
            more hold by one of them through `anyOf`,
            `anyOf (eq 5) (eq 10) (eq 15)`.
        ~~|
    | proves :isTrueWhenOneIsTrue
        |~~ False or true is true. ~~|
        ~(false | or true)
    | proves :isFalseWhenNeitherHolds
        |~~ False or false is false. ~~|
        ~(false | or false | not)
    | proves :computesTheConditionAgainstTheSubject
        |~~ The condition is computed against the subject, a boolean. ~~|
        ~(false | or (not))
    | proves :condition/KindNotTakenError/refusesANumber
        |~~ A condition that is no boolean is refused under the input. ~~|
        ~(false | or 1 !| get [~(type) /actualType] | eq [::boolean/or/condition/KindNotTakenError ::number])
    | proves :SubjectNotBooleanError/refusesANumber
        |~~ A subject that is no boolean is refused under the verb. ~~|
        ~(1 | or true !| get [~(type) /actualType] | eq [::boolean/or/SubjectNotBooleanError ::number])
    | proves :ArgsBeyondInputsError/refusesTwoBooleans
        |~~ A third condition is refused under the verb. ~~|
        ~(false | or true true !| type | eq ::boolean/or/ArgsBeyondInputsError)
    | proves :asksTheConditionOfTheBoolean
        |~~ The condition is asked of the boolean the step before answered,
            so a comparison of the element there is refused.
        ~~|
        ~([1 2 3] | filter ~(gt 1 | or (lt 3)) !| get [~(type) /leftType] | eq [::call/KindsMismatchedError ::boolean])
    | impl ::builtin{}

:boolean/toString
  |~~ Answers the literal of the boolean as a string, `true | toString`
      answering `"true"`.
      ::link~(::string/toString | doc)
  ~~|
  ::fn
    | returns |~~ `"true"` or `"false"`. ~~| ::string
    | proves :writesTheLiteral
        |~~ A boolean answers its literal. ~~|
        ~([true false] | map ~toString | eq ["true" "false"])
    | impl ::builtin{}

:boolean/toKeyword
  |~~ Answers the keyword of the boolean's literal, `true | toKeyword`
      answering `:true`, as `groupBy` files the elements a condition
      parts, `[1 2 3] | groupBy ~(gt 1)`.
      ::link~(::vec/groupBy | doc)
  ~~|
  ::fn
    | returns |~~ `:true` or `:false`. ~~| ::keyword
    | proves :readsTheKeyGroupByFiledItUnder
        |~~ A boolean turns into the key `groupBy` files a condition
            under.
        ~~|
        ~([1 2 3] | groupBy ~(gt 1) | query (true | toKeyword) | eq [2 3])
    | impl ::builtin{}

